# NestJS Clean Architecture Standards

## Architecture Overview

The project must follow Clean Architecture principles.

Dependency flow:

```txt
Presentation
    ↓
Application
    ↓
Domain

Infrastructure
    ↑
```

Dependencies must always point inward.

---

## Module Structure

Every feature module must follow:

```txt
src/modules/<module-name>/
├── domain/
│   ├── entities/
│   ├── value-objects/
│   ├── enums/
│   └── repositories/
│
├── application/
│   ├── dto/
│   ├── use-cases/
│   ├── interfaces/
│   └── mappers/
│
├── infrastructure/
│   ├── persistence/
│   │   ├── entities/
│   │   └── repositories/
│   ├── providers/
│   ├── services/
│   ├── guards/
│   └── strategies/
│
├── presentation/
│   ├── controllers/
│   ├── requests/
│   └── responses/
│
└── <module>.module.ts
```

---

## Domain Layer

Domain contains business entities and business rules.

Allowed:

- Entities
- Value Objects
- Enums
- Repository Contracts
- Domain Events

Forbidden:

- NestJS decorators
- TypeORM decorators
- Prisma models
- HTTP logic
- Database queries
- JWT logic
- Redis logic
- External APIs

Example:

```ts
export class User {
  constructor(
    public readonly id: string,
    public email: string,
  ) {}
}
```

Never use:

```ts
@Entity()
export class User {}
```

inside the Domain layer.

---

## Application Layer

Application contains Use Cases.

Each use case must represent a single business action.

Naming:

```txt
create-user.use-case.ts
update-user.use-case.ts
delete-user.use-case.ts
login.use-case.ts
refresh-token.use-case.ts
```

Use cases should expose:

```ts
execute()
```

Example:

```ts
export class CreateUserUseCase {
  async execute(dto: CreateUserDto) {}
}
```

Forbidden:

- TypeORM Repository
- Prisma Client
- Raw SQL
- Axios
- HTTP requests

Application must depend only on abstractions.

---

## Repository Pattern

Repository interfaces belong to:

```txt
domain/repositories
```

Example:

```ts
export interface IUserRepository {
  findById(id: string): Promise<User | null>;
  create(user: User): Promise<User>;
}
```

Repository implementations belong to:

```txt
infrastructure/persistence/repositories
```

Example:

```ts
export class UserRepository
  implements IUserRepository {}
```

---

## Infrastructure Layer

Infrastructure contains all external integrations.

Examples:

- TypeORM
- PostgreSQL
- Redis
- JWT
- RabbitMQ
- Kafka
- AWS S3
- Cloudinary
- Mail Providers

Infrastructure implements contracts defined by Domain/Application.

Business logic must never be placed here.

---

## Presentation Layer

Presentation handles communication with clients.

Allowed:

- Controllers
- GraphQL Resolvers
- WebSocket Gateways
- Request Validation
- Response Mapping

Controllers must remain thin.

Example:

```ts
@Post()
create(@Body() dto: CreateUserDto) {
  return this.createUserUseCase.execute(dto);
}
```

Never:

```ts
@Post()
async create() {
  return this.userRepository.save(...);
}
```

---

## Entity Separation

Always separate:

### Domain Entity

```txt
domain/entities/user.ts
```

### Database Entity

```txt
infrastructure/persistence/entities/user.entity.ts
```

Never use TypeORM entities as domain entities.

---

## Dependency Injection

Use constructor injection only.

Example:

```ts
constructor(
  private readonly createUserUseCase: CreateUserUseCase,
) {}
```

Avoid service locator patterns.

---

## Shared Folder

Shared contains reusable cross-module components.

Allowed:

```txt
shared/constants
shared/decorators
shared/exceptions
shared/filters
shared/guards
shared/interceptors
shared/pipes
shared/types
shared/utils
```

Forbidden:

```txt
shared/user.service.ts
shared/auth.service.ts
shared/order.use-case.ts
```

Business logic must remain inside feature modules.

---

## Naming Conventions

Files:

```txt
create-user.use-case.ts
update-user.use-case.ts

user.repository.ts
user.entity.ts

auth.controller.ts

jwt.strategy.ts
roles.guard.ts
```

Classes:

```txt
CreateUserUseCase
UpdateUserUseCase

UserRepository

AuthController

JwtStrategy
RolesGuard
```

---

## Golden Rules

1. Controllers must not contain business logic.
2. Repositories must not contain business rules.
3. Business logic belongs only in Use Cases.
4. Domain must be framework-independent.
5. Infrastructure implements abstractions.
6. Use dependency inversion everywhere possible.
7. Never inject TypeORM repositories directly into Controllers.
8. Never access the database from the Presentation layer.
9. One Use Case = One Business Action.
10. Keep modules isolated and feature-based.